/// Reset the clock chip.  (V36)
///
/// This routine does whatever is neeeded to put the clock chip
/// into a working and usable state and also sets the date on the
/// clock chip to 01-Jan-1978.
///
/// # Synopsis
///
/// ResetBattClock( )
///
/// void ResetBattClock( void );
///
/// # Notes
///
/// # Bugs
///
/// FS-UAE services `ReadBattClock` from the host clock and discards writes, so on the
/// emulated A4000 used for verification the battery clock is effectively read-only.
/// Live verification shows `WriteBattClock` and `ResetBattClock` returning normally while
/// a following `ReadBattClock` still reports the live host time, never the written value
/// and never the 01-Jan-1978 epoch. Real hardware stores both. Tier 3 preserves whatever
/// the machine does.
@library("battclock.resource")
extern pub fn ResetBattClock()
/// Read time from clock chip.  (V36)
///
/// This routine reads the time from the clock chip and returns it
/// as the number of seconds from 01-jan-1978.
///
/// # Synopsis
///
/// AmigaTime = ReadBattClock( )
///
/// ULONG ReadBattClock( void );
/// D0
///
/// # Returns
///
/// AmigaTime       The number of seconds from 01-Jan-1978 that
/// the clock chip thinks it is.
///
/// # Notes
///
/// If the clock chip returns an invalid date, the clock chip is
/// reset and 0 is returned.
@library("battclock.resource")
extern pub fn ReadBattClock() -> u32
/// Set the time on the clock chip.  (V36)
///
/// This routine writes the time given in AmigaTime to the clock
/// chip.
///
/// # Synopsis
///
/// WriteBattClock( AmigaTime )
///                 D0
///
/// void WriteBattClock( ULONG );
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * AmigaTime       The number of seconds from 01-Jan-1978 to the time that should be written to the clock chip.
///
/// # Notes
///
/// # Bugs
///
/// FS-UAE services `ReadBattClock` from the host clock and discards writes, so on the
/// emulated A4000 used for verification the battery clock is effectively read-only.
/// Live verification shows `WriteBattClock` and `ResetBattClock` returning normally while
/// a following `ReadBattClock` still reports the live host time, never the written value
/// and never the 01-Jan-1978 epoch. Real hardware stores both. Tier 3 preserves whatever
/// the machine does.
@library("battclock.resource")
extern pub fn WriteBattClock(time: u32)
